{"step_index":1607,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T23:58:30Z","thinking":"**Investigating Unexpected Large W values**\n\nObserving cases where `rad >= 2*k2`, the output reveals that $W$ is significantly greater than `rad + 1`. This discrepancy suggests that $W$'s size is not directly tied to the radius in these situations.\n\nThe hypothesis is that the remaining vertices in $V$ contribute to the large value of $W$ when $k_2$ is relatively small.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"tail -n 8 /tmp/agyhome.NWyWnj/.gemini/antigravity-cli/brain/f458c9cf-488d-4cd8-8365-7c4e4b16e3f9/.system_generated/logs/transcript.jsonl","Cwd":"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/handoff/promising13/task_e20263a9","RunPersistent":false,"WaitMsBeforeAsync":5000,"toolAction":"Viewing conductor transcript","toolSummary":"View conductor transcript"}}]}
{"step_index":1608,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T23:58:34Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-12T01:58:34+02:00\nCompleted At: 2026-09-12T01:58:34+02:00\n\nThe command exited with code 0.\nOutput:\n<truncated 3 lines>\n{\"step_index\":1592,\"source\":\"MODEL\",\"type\":\"GENERIC\",\"status\":\"DONE\",\"created_at\":\"2026-09-11T23:58:22Z\",\"content\":\"Created At: 2026-09-12T01:58:22+02:00\\nCompleted At: 2026-09-12T01:58:22+02:00\\n\\nThe command exited with code 0.\\nOutput:\\nTesting if W=4 can ever have radius >= 4...\\r\\nPROVED: when W=4, radius <= 3 ALWAYS!\\r\\n\\n\"}\r\n{\"step_index\":1593,\"source\":\"MODEL\",\"type\":\"PLANNER_RESPONSE\",\"status\":\"DONE\",\"created_at\":\"2026-09-11T23:58:22Z\",\"thinking\":\"**Evaluating radius bound constraints**\\n\\nIf the width, *W*, is 4, then radius is at most 3, always. If *W* is 5 or greater and the radius is less than or equal to 4, then the radius + 1 is at most 5, which is less than or equal to *W*. This holds true in both cases of *W* = 4 and when *W* is >= 5.\\n\\nTherefore, for all graphs where the radius is less than or equal to 4, radius + 1 will always be less than or equal to *W*. The analysis now focuses on graphs with radius <= 5.\\n\\n\\n**Refining radius and width bounds**\\n\\nFor any *k*, if *W* <= 2*k*, the radius cannot be >= 2*k* because the maximum radius is bounded by floor(3W/4), which is less than W. The goal now is to prove this using theorem proving.  The existing approach, relying on a `sorry`, needs to be refined. The challenge is in proving the case where the radius is greater than 3.\\n\\n\\n\",\"tool_calls\":[{\"name\":\"view_file\",\"args\":{\"AbsolutePath\":\"\\\"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/handoff/promising13/task_e20263a9/test_card_bound.lean\\\"\",\"ContentOffset\":\"0\",\"EndLine\":\"100\",\"StartLine\":\"1\",\"toolAction\":\"\\\"View test_card_bound.lean\\\"\",\"toolSummary\":\"\\\"View test_card_bound.lean\\\"\"}