{"step_index":1611,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T23:58:47Z","thinking":"**Deriving Radius Lower Bound**\n\nEstablishing a connection between the graph's radius and the sum of vertex function values, focusing on layers $L_0$ to $L_R$ around a central vertex $c$, where $R$ is the radius. The goal is to prove the function value sum is at least radius $G + 1$. Each vertex possesses a value of 0, 1, or 2; the key condition is that every vertex with a value of zero needs a neighbor with a value of two.\n\n\n**Analyzing Layer Contributions**\n\nFocusing on the layers $L_k$, if a layer contains only vertices with a value of zero, those vertices must connect to vertices with a value of two, which must be in adjacent layers. Thus, layers with zero contribution require at least one neighbor with a value of two. It appears, this results in an effective average contribution of at least one per two adjacent layers.\n\nThis constraint poses a problem when trying to ensure two consecutive layers are zero, therefore, exploring the effects of having multiple consecutive zero-value layers on this approach. The implications of this would violate closed neighborhood conditions.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"tail -n 8 /tmp/agyhome.NWyWnj/.gemini/antigravity-cli/brain/f458c9cf-488d-4cd8-8365-7c4e4b16e3f9/.system_generated/logs/transcript.jsonl","Cwd":"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/handoff/promising13/task_e20263a9","RunPersistent":false,"WaitMsBeforeAsync":5000,"toolAction":"Viewing conductor transcript tail","toolSummary":"View conductor transcript tail"}}]}
